itle":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The generator curve $E$","parts":[["text","The generator curve "],["math","\\(E\\)"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#generator-curve"},{"id":"model/field/generator-point-equation","title":"The coordinates of $P_0$ satisfy the curve equation","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.genP0_equation","summary":"Let $x_0=\\frac{1641-1160s}{8}$ and $y_0=\\frac{-123720+87483s}{32}$ in $K_0$. Then and $y_0\\ne0$.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The coordinates of $P_0$ satisfy the curve equation","parts":[["text","The coordinates of "],["math","\\(P_0\\)"],["text"," satisfy the curve equation"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#generator-point-equation"},{"id":"model/field/generator-point","title":"The point $P_0$","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.genP0","summary":"The point $P_0\\in E(K_0)$ is the affine point =\\left(\\tfrac{1641}{8}-145s,\\ -\\tfrac{15465}{4}+\\tfrac{87483}{32}s\\right).$$ Its four rational coordinates are $\\frac{1641}{8},-145,-\\frac{15465}{4},\\frac{87483}{32}$.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The point $P_0$","parts":[["text","The point "],["math","\\(P_0\\)"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#generator-point"},{"id":"model/field/point-code-properties","title":"Elementary properties of codes","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.pointCode_injective, ZInQ.iota_add","summary":"1. The map $\\operatorname{code}:E(K_0)\\to\\mathbb Q^5$ is injective. The first coordinate of a code is $1$ exactly for $O$ and $0$ for every affine point. 2. For every point $Q$, $\\operatorname{code}(-Q)$ is $\\operatorname{code}(Q)$ with its last two coordinates negated. In particular $\\iota(0)=(1,0,0,0,0)$ and, for every $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, $\\iota(-n)$ is $\\iota(n)$ with its last two coordinates negated. 3.…","route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#point-code-properties"},{"id":"model/field/division-polynomial-multiples","title":"Division polynomials compute multiples","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.mulByN_formulas","summary":"The multiplication-by-$n$ formulas hold for $E$ over $K_0$: for every affine point $P=(x,y)\\in E(K_0)$ and every integer $n\\ne0$,","route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#division-polynomial-multiples"},{"id":"model/field/fixed-point-codes","title":"Fixed point codes by division polynomials","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.iota_eq_psi_unconditional, ZInQ.iota_eq_psi","summary":"For every integer $n\\ge1$, $\\psi_n(P_0)\\ne0$ and \\operatorname{im}y_n\\bigr),\\qquad x_n=x(P_0)-\\frac{\\psi_{n+1}(P_0)\\psi_{n-1}(P_0)}{\\psi_n(P_0)^2},\\quad y_n=\\frac{\\psi_{2n}(P_0)}{2\\psi_n(P_0)^4},$$ where $\\operatorname{re}(u+vs)=u$, $\\operatorname{im}(u+vs)=v$ and the $\\psi_k$ are the division polynomials of $E$ ($A=-11243+7950s$). For $n\\ge1$, $\\iota(-n)$ is obtained from $\\iota(n)$ by negating its last two…","route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#fixed-point-codes"},{"id":"model/field/integers-pid","title":"$\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$ is a principal ideal domain","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Instance","lean":"ZInQ.Zs.isPrincipalIdealRing, ZInQ.Zs.uniqueFactorizationMonoid","summary":"The ring $\\mathbb Z[s]$ is a principal ideal domain (the ring of integers of $K_0$ has class number one). Consequently it is a unique factorization domain with fraction field $K_0$: every $x\\in K_0^\\times$ is $x=u\\prod_\\pi\\pi^{v_\\pi(x)}$ with $u\\in\\mathbb Z[s]^\\times$, $\\pi$ running over a set of representatives of the prime elements up to units, and integers $v_\\pi(x)$, almost all zero, uniquely determined by $x$.…","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"$\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$ is a principal ideal domain","parts":[["math","\\(\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]\\)"],["text"," is a principal ideal domain"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#integers-pid"},{"id":"model/field/units","title":"Units of $\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Zs_unit_sqClass, ZInQ.isSquare_of_totallyPositive_unit","summary":"1. $\\mathbb Z[s]^\\times=\\{\\pm\\epsilon^k:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$ with $\\epsilon=1+s$, and $\\epsilon^{-1}=s-1$, $\\operatorname N(\\epsilon)=-1$. 2. The unit squareclasses $\\mathbb Z[s]^\\times/(\\mathbb Z[s]^\\times)^2$ are exactly the four classes of $1,-1,\\epsilon,-\\epsilon$, whose sign vectors are $(+,+)$, $(-,-)$, $(+,-)$ and $(-,+)$ respectively. 3. Every totally positive unit is the square of a unit.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"Units of $\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$","parts":[["text","Units of "],["math","\\(\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]\\)"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#units"},{"id":"model/field/residue-checks","title":"Residue computations modulo seven","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.residues_mod_seven, ZInQ.not_isSquare_neg_one_zmod_seven","summary":"The following closed statements about integers modulo $7$ hold:","route":"../roadmap/model/field/index.html#residue-checks"},{"id":"model/field/special-primes","title":"The primes $(s)$ and $(d)$ of $\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.prime_sqrt2Z, ZInQ.prime_genDZ, ZInQ.vS_formula_withTop","summary":"1. $s$ is a prime element of $\\mathbb Z[s]$, $2=s^2$, and $\\mathbb Z[s]/(s)\\cong\\mathbb F_2$ via $a_0+a_1s\\mapsto a_0\\bmod2$. For $c_0,c_1\\in\\mathbb Q$ not both zero, with $v_2$ the $2$-adic valuation on $\\mathbb Q$ and $v_2(0)=+\\infty$. 2. $d=75-53s$ is a prime element with $\\operatorname N(d)=7$, and $\\mathbb Z[s]/(d)\\cong\\mathbb F_7$ via $a_0+a_1s\\mapsto a_0+3a_1\\bmod7$. In this residue field $-1$ is not a…","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The primes $(s)$ and $(d)$ of $\\mathbb Z[\\sqrt2]$","parts":[["text","The primes "],["math","\\((s)\\)"],["text"," and "],["math","\\((d)\\)"],["text"," of "],["math","\\(